TASK: Build a comprehensive testing package for the PoRJE repository and use it to
audit the project top to bottom. This is a build-and-verify task, not a fix task.
The deliverable is (a) a test suite that proves every claim the project makes,
and (b) a written audit report with evidence for each claim.

You will not implement new features. You will not refactor production code except
where a test proves it broken. You will not weaken any existing test. When a test
exposes a bug, you will report it, then fix it only if the fix is a straightforward
correction of behavior the code already claims to have.

============================================================
GROUND RULES
============================================================

1. Every test file you create goes in jac/tests/ with a *_tests.jac suffix
   (the 0.37-compatible naming). Do not use test_*.jac — it collides with
   Python's test-module import machinery.

2. Copy the spawn idiom verbatim from an existing passing test in the repo.
   Do not invent spawn syntax. If you are unsure how a walker runs in tests,
   open one of the 15 existing tests and mirror it exactly.

3. Every new test must be runnable via `jac test -d jac/tests` and must pass.
   If a test fails because the code is wrong, fix the code (with a minimal
   change) and re-run. If a test fails because the test is wrong, fix the test.

4. Every test that touches the on-chain path must use the evm_spy — never
   broadcast a real transaction during the test suite. Real transactions are
   reserved for the manual e2e run in Phase 7.

5. Do not modify fixtures to make a test pass. If a fixture is wrong, fix the
   fixture and adjust every test that depended on the wrong value.

6. Report findings as you go. At the end, produce a single AUDIT.md at the repo
   root with the evidence table specified in Phase 12.

