PHASE 5 — THE SIZING CALCULATION (CRITICAL)
============================================================

Create jac/tests/sizing_tests.jac.

This phase proves the Cover walker's justified_amount calculation is correct.
This is the Jac-native sizing step that determines how much Act is allowed to
mint. Every demo claim about "how much is justified" reduces to this formula.

5.1 — State the formula explicitly
  In a comment at the top of the file, write the exact formula:
    coverage_value   = reserve.amount * price.value
    liability_value  = (liab.minted_units + liab.demo_position) * price.value
    ratio            = coverage_value / liability_value
    max_mintable     = (coverage_value / price.value) - liab.minted_units - liab.demo_position
    justified        = max(0.0, max_mintable)

  Assert this matches the code in cover.jac line-for-line. If the code differs,
  report and fix.

5.2 — Exact-fit case
  Setup: reserve.amount = 1_000_000, price.value = 1.0,
         liab.minted_units = 1_000_000, liab.demo_position = 0.
  Spawn Cover.
  Assert color == "green" (ratio 1.0 meets min_coverage_ratio 1.00).
  Assert justified_amount == 0.0 (no room to mint more).

5.3 — Surplus case
  Setup: reserve.amount = 1_500_000, price.value = 1.0,
         liab.minted_units = 1_000_000, liab.demo_position = 0.
  Spawn Cover.
  Assert color == "green".
  Assert justified_amount == 500_000.0 (exactly the surplus).
  Assert coverage_ratio == 1.5.

5.4 — Thin case (yellow)
  Setup: reserve.amount = 970_000, price.value = 1.0,
         liab.minted_units = 1_000_000, liab.demo_position = 0.
  Spawn Cover.
  Assert color == "yellow" (0.97 is between floor 0.95 and min 1.00).
  Assert justified_amount == 0.0 (nothing above 1.00 means no surplus).

5.5 — Underfloor case (red)
  Setup: reserve.amount = 900_000, price.value = 1.0,
         liab.minted_units = 1_000_000, liab.demo_position = 0.
  Spawn Cover.
  Assert color == "red" (0.90 < floor 0.95).

5.6 — Price is applied correctly
  Setup: reserve.amount = 1_500_000, price.value = 2.0,
         liab.minted_units = 1_000_000, liab.demo_position = 0.
  Spawn Cover.
  Coverage_value = 3_000_000; liability_value = 2_000_000; ratio = 1.5.
  max_mintable = (3_000_000 / 2.0) - 1_000_000 - 0 = 1_500_000 - 1_000_000 = 500_000.
  Assert justified_amount == 500_000.0.
  Assert coverage_ratio == 1.5.
  This proves the price cancels correctly in the numerator and is applied
  correctly in the ratio.

5.7 — demo_position is included in liability
  Setup: reserve.amount = 1_500_000, price.value = 1.0,
         liab.minted_units = 1_000_000, liab.demo_position = 500_000.
  Spawn Cover.
  Assert liability is treated as 1_500_000 (minted + demo).
  ratio = 1_000_000 / 1_500_000 = 0.6667.
  Assert color == "red" (below floor).
  This proves demo_position is not silently ignored.

5.8 — Zero liability is guarded
  Setup: reserve.amount = 1_000_000, price.value = 1.0,
         liab.minted_units = 0, liab.demo_position = 0.
  Spawn Cover.
  The code has a guard: `ratio = coverage_value / liability_value if liability_value > 0.0 else 0.0`.
  Assert color is NOT green (ratio 0.0 < min_coverage_ratio).
  Assert no exception is raised.
  Assert justified_amount == 1_000_000.0 (the full reserve is mintable because
  there is no liability to subtract).

5.9 — Zero reserve is handled
  Setup: reserve.amount = 0, price.value = 1.0,
         liab.minted_units = 1_000_000, liab.demo_position = 0.
  Spawn Cover.
  Assert color == "red".
  Assert justified_amount == 0.0.

5.10 — Act mints exactly min(requested, justified)
  For each pair (requested, justified, expected_mint):
    (100,       500_000, 100)
    (500_000,   500_000, 500_000)
    (900_000,   500_000, 500_000)
    (1_000_000, 0,       refuse)
    (0,         500_000, refuse — requested is zero)
    (-100,      500_000, refuse — negative requested)
  Use the evm_spy. Assert spy_mint_call_count() is 1 for the first three,
  0 for the last three. Assert the spy's amount argument equals expected_mint
  when the spy is called.

5.11 — Sizing is deterministic across price scaling
  Run the same logical setup three times with price = 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, and
  reserve/liability scaled proportionally. Assert justified_amount is identical
  in all three cases (within float tolerance 1e-6). This proves the pricing
  cancels and the sizing is in reserve units, not price units.

5.12 — Summary print
  Script `scripts/verify_sizing.py`:
    Runs 5.2 through 5.11 against the live backend. Prints a table:
      case_name | reserve | price | minted | demo | expected_justified | actual_justified | PASS/FAIL
    Exits nonzero if any case fails.
  This script is the "isn't there supposed to be a calculation by Jac to size
  the trade" confirmation. Its output is the evidence.

